# TODO - Configurable accelerator algorithm adapters

## Objective

Separate optimization/search strategy from accelerator hardware. Define common
algorithm ports for enumerative, stochastic, Monte Carlo, evolutionary, learned,
hybrid, pruning, and future strategies, and let CPU, CUDA, ROCm, or later
hardware adapters provide execution capacity for those strategies. Select
algorithms and hardware through deterministic configuration with optional CLI
overrides, record the exact combination in benchmark evidence, and permit side-
by-side comparison without recompiling or modifying compiler semantics.

## Acceptance

- Hardware backend and search/optimization strategy are independently selected;
  unsupported combinations fail explicitly and benchmark identity records both
  dimensions.
- A CPU/reference path remains sufficient for correctness, and accelerator
  failure/unavailability changes performance rather than semantic acceptance.
- The declared scope contains no unresolved placeholder implementation or
  undocumented workaround required for this objective to function.
- Evidence is durable enough to move this TODO to `docs/todo/completed/` and
  remove the exact TODO heading without losing unfinished intent.

## Evidence

- Expected durable artifact surface: `accelerator/`, `algorithms/`,
  `optimizer/`, `benchmarks/accelerator/`, `tests/optimizer/`.
- Required evidence: CPU/reference differential results, device/resource
  metadata, failure/fallback tests, and benchmark samples for claimed speedups.
- Active foundation: `SearchRequest` records algorithm ID, deterministic seed,
  evaluation budget, and problem bytes independently from backend capability.
  `CpuSearchExecutionAdapter` supplies portable execution capacity; optional
  backend failure or malformed shape falls back to it. Search proposals and
  verification hints remain untrusted, and only `TrustedCandidateVerifier` can
  admit a proposal. `SearchSelection` resolves independent algorithm/backend
  bindings with explicit overrides, rejects unsupported combinations, and records
  configured versus actual backend identity after fallback. Versioned search
  configuration v1 now loads independent algorithm/backend identities from TOML,
  rejects unknown/empty configuration, preserves source identity, and applies
  explicit caller overrides without mutating the loaded base selection. The
  deterministic corpus enumerator is the first concrete CPU-only strategy.
  `classic-rotate-target-search-v1` additionally composes one bounded strategy with
  interchangeable CPU/CUDA candidate evaluators. Live CUDA matches CPU proposals
  over 257 candidates, records configured/actual backend identity, and still uses
  independent CPU admission. `python -m optimizer.cli` now loads versioned search
  configuration and canonical problem bytes, applies explicit overrides, and
  emits deterministic JSON with problem hash, configured/actual backend identity,
  device metadata, seed/budget, and explicitly untrusted proposals. Supported
  CUDA setup failure preserves configured CUDA intent while falling back to CPU;
  unsupported algorithm/backend pairs fail explicitly. The first retained
  Benchmark Protocol v1 comparison covers the complete 59,049-word classic
  domain with 15 samples per backend. CPU median is 401.185 ms and CUDA median is
  412.570 ms on the RTX 4060, so the 0.972x CUDA/CPU result rejects the speedup
  hypothesis while preserving exact proposals and independent CPU admission. A
  separate 15-profile run explains 97.5% of CPU and 99.5% of CUDA median total
  time through named phases. CUDA host-side phases account for about 57.0%, and
  batch construction plus proposal selection consume about 173.081 ms. Reusable
  validated/prepared search state is now active. One immutable proof binds the
  validated request/batch to exact algorithm, batch-builder, and selector identity;
  matching CPU/CUDA adapters can reuse it, while forged or mismatched strategy
  state fails closed. Prepared execution retains verifier-only acceptance and
  removes repeated batch build/validation from the amortized path. A four-route
  retained ordinary/prepared evidence records 293.564/148.590 ms CPU medians
  (1.976x) and 306.872/162.693 ms CUDA medians (1.886x). Prepared CUDA remains
  about 9.5% slower than prepared CPU, and preparation is outside timed intervals.
  The prepared-path profile attributes 79.9% of CPU and 81.2% of CUDA median total
  time to backend evaluation, with proposal selection at 19.6% and 18.7%.
  Fixed-width packed candidate evidence now implements that boundary. One opaque
  byte buffer inherits logical IDs from validated request order; generic item
  results remain compatible, malformed packed shape fails closed, and search avoids
  per-candidate evidence objects. Verification-assist materializes only at the hint
  boundary. Retained packed evidence lowers CPU ordinary/prepared medians to
  211.693/77.309 ms and CUDA medians to 230.144/91.199 ms. Packed backend
  evaluation falls 2.326x on CPU and 2.058x on CUDA, while packed CUDA prepared
  remains about 18.0% slower than CPU. Prepared candidate execution now stores one
  validated decoded state under exact preparer identity. Rotate search reuses a
  hardware-neutral `PrimitiveBatch` through matching CPU/CUDA evaluators; forged or
  mismatched state fails closed, and generic strategies remain compatible.
  Retained prepared medians are 43.129 ms CPU and 57.296 ms CUDA,
  1.792x/1.592x faster than the packed baseline. Ordinary routes regress
  6.6%/3.7%, and CUDA remains 32.8% slower than CPU prepared. Exact primitive
  preparation now carries sealed validation proof. CUDA prepared execution retains
  one proof-bound input/output allocation, rebuilds on proof identity change, and
  reports build/evaluation/reuse/resident counters. Ordinary execution remains
  one-shot. Retained CUDA prepared median is 34.132 ms versus 46.232 ms CPU
  (1.355x), and CUDA improves 1.679x over its pre-resident baseline. CUDA backend
  evaluation improves 3.252x, but total phase time does not because selection rises
  to 46.331 ms. Prepared state now includes an immutable exact membership index;
  CPU/CUDA proposal checks reuse 59,049 validated identity/payload pairs instead of
  rebuilding a dictionary. Ordinary execution stays one-shot and forged payloads
  fail closed. Retained prepared medians are 26.797 ms CPU and 17.970 ms CUDA,
  1.725x/1.899x faster than the resident baseline; selection improves
  3.519x/3.939x. Improved controls bound attribution. Packed evidence scanning is
  next. Synthesis/guided
  strategies, ROCm search implementations, richer orchestration,
  and broader representative benchmarks remain open.
